  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure overhead this morning will move \noff the New England Coast by tonight. Mid and high level\nmoisture will overspread the area today with increasing cloud \ncover while low level dry air is slow to overcome. The upper \nlevel ridge axis will expand into western New York today but \nwill be suppressed slightly to the south tonight as a trough \nswings across northern Ontario into Quebec. Expect to see \nscattered light showers develop tonight. Can not rule out a \nthunderstorm or two but instability is limited and mainly \nconfined to areas east of Lake Erie.\n\nSurface low pressure tracks from near Hudson Bay to northern Quebec \nwith the trailing frontal boundary settling south into the area on \nWednesday. High temperatures are expected to reach the upper 70s but \nmay be impacted by coverage of showers. CAPE values peak in the 500-\n1000 J/kg range across the southern tier and a few thunderstorms may \ntry to develop late afternoon. Pops are generally in the chance\nrange as placement could end up just a little farther south \ninto PA.\n\nModels do show some spread by Thursday morning with regards to where \nthis boundary may be located. It very well could lift back north \nduring the day on Thursday with additional showers before strong \nhigh pressure builds south out of Ontario with the airmass drying \nout for Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Strong high pressure will build south out of Canada \non Friday night. Light wind conditions are expected towards the \nAdirondacks while better chances for clouds are focused in SW New \nYork. While most of the area can expect lows in the upper 40s \nto near 50 degrees, portions of the Tug Hill may see lows fall \nto near 40 degrees with a few cooler spots if ideal radiational cooling\nconditions develop.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Low pressure will track out of the Plains across the \nCentral and Eastern Great Lakes on Saturday. This will bring a \nlittle more focused potential for showers and possibly \nthunderstorms, Saturday night into Sunday. Probabilities of half \ninch of rain or more are generally in the 50-60 percent range.",
